      "key": "a permuted-row gather of a small per-row operand (scale, bias, routing metadata) re-read inside the k-loop of a fused-MoE grouped GEMM on gfx950",
      "lifecycle": "active",
      "type": "lever",
      "confidence": "★★",
      "effect": "+11.5% geomean on top of an already-converged stack, the largest single gain after the first round and 5x the prize the same operand's in-loop amortisation had been priced at; per-case +14.8% and +14.3% on the two large token counts, ~+1% on the smallest token count (which was already at ~92% of its compulsory-bandwidth roof). Run end state director-verified 58.56x geomean, 32.3x / 78.1x / 79.6x from smallest to largest token count, correctness PASS.",
      "title": "Pre-lay a strided per-iteration operand in a prologue kernel, in the consumer's reading order",
      "lever": "When a small per-row operand (scale, bias, routing metadata) is re-read inside the k-loop through a permuted-row gather with a large stride, stop trying to amortise the gather inside the loop and change WHO produces it: a tiny prologue kernel writes the operand once into scratch in the consumer's access order (expanded rows, k-major) so the inner read is a short run of consecutive elements. The payoff is L1/L2 REQUEST count, and it can be several times what an in-loop estimate predicts, because the gather also holds address registers and serialises against the main operand staging.",
      "apply": "Keep it as a separate dispatch writing a persistent per-device scratch buffer whose contents are recomputed every call; drive the consumer off the pre-laid buffer behind a default-on switch with the old path as fallback. Then re-sweep num_stages and the chunking knobs, since the loop body has changed shape.",
      "verify": "The strong signature is that VMEM/LDS/MFMA instruction counts stay bit-identical while L1/L2 request counts fall. Confirm the prologue dispatch and its scratch traffic are inside the timed region before quoting the delta.",
      "pitfall": "the L2 HIT RATE dropped while the kernel got faster -> the win deletes redundant hits while misses stay constant -> rank candidates on wall clock, not on hit rate.",
      "caution": "Also verify the prologue's own cost separately (here 1.3-3.1% of kernel time, unhideable because the consumer owns every CU), and re-check whether an earlier in-loop amortisation of the same operand has become dead code — a superseded path that is still present reads as still applied in any summary that counts diffs rather than executed paths.",
